-- Passage rg4-main-idea-summary-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('623f3c58-e6ba-5a0b-bc8c-52aee748a175', 'rg4-main-idea-summary-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g4-main-idea-summary'), null,
        '3-5', $q$How Smart Traffic Lights Work$q$, $q$Some traffic lights change on a timer. They turn green and red after the same number of seconds, all day long. But many newer traffic lights are smarter. They can sense cars and change when they are needed.

One common tool is a loop of wire buried under the road. Electricity runs through the wire. When a car stops on top of it, the metal in the car changes the signal. The traffic light then knows a car is waiting.

Other lights use small cameras mounted on poles. A computer looks at the pictures and counts the cars in each lane. If one street has a long line, the light can stay green there a little longer.

Smart lights help people in several ways. Drivers spend less time sitting at empty red lights. Cars waste less fuel while waiting. Many crossings also have buttons for people walking. When someone presses the button, the light knows to give them time to cross.

Engineers keep improving these systems. In some cities, nearby lights share information with each other. That way, a line of cars can roll through several green lights in a row.$q$, 'draft')
